    worked:[
      { lbl:'Name it + reassure', txt:'"What you\u2019re describing \u2014 stereotyped attacks of central tummy pain with pallor and sickness lasting hours, then completely well for weeks, with your own migraines in the background \u2014 sounds like abdominal migraine. It\u2019s a recognised form of migraine in children, and it\u2019s real, not \u2018nothing\u2019."' },
      { lbl:'Why tests are normal', txt:'"That\u2019s exactly why the tests keep coming back normal \u2014 it\u2019s a diagnosis we make from the pattern after ruling out other causes, not something a blood test shows. Normal results actually fit and are reassuring."' },
      { lbl:'Red-flag check', txt:'"Let me just check a few things that would make me think differently: is she losing weight or off her growth? Does the pain or vomiting wake her at night, is the vomit ever green, any blood in her stool or vomit, fevers, or pain always in one spot? … None of those — reassuring. I can’t examine her over the phone, though, so I’d like to see her in person to examine her tummy, check her height and weight on her growth chart, and test a urine sample."' },
      { lbl:'Exclude mimics', txt:'"I\u2019ll make sure constipation, a urine infection and coeliac have been considered, as those can mimic it \u2014 a urine test and a coeliac blood test if not done."' },
      { lbl:'Manage', txt:'"For attacks: rest in a quiet, dark room, sips of fluid, and paracetamol or ibuprofen early in the attack. Anti-sickness medicines aren’t routinely used at her age, and migraine tablets like triptans aren’t licensed for her. Keep a diary to spot triggers \u2014 stress, missed meals, poor sleep, dehydration. If they’re frequent or disabling, I’d involve a paediatrician, who can decide about preventive options."' },
      { lbl:'Safety-net', txt:'"If she ever vomits green, call 999 or go to A&E — that’s an emergency. Come back — or seek urgent help — if the pattern changes, she loses weight, the pain stays in one place or wakes her, you see blood, or she\u2019s unwell between attacks. Otherwise this often improves with age, sometimes turning into ordinary migraines later."' },
    ],
    learning:'ABDOMINAL MIGRAINE is a recognised paediatric variant of migraine: recurrent, STEREOTYPED episodes of moderate-to-severe MIDLINE/central abdominal pain lasting hours (ICHD-3, international: 2–72 hours untreated; Rome IV, international: at least 1 hour) with associated PALLOR, nausea/vomiting, anorexia and lethargy, and \u2014 crucially \u2014 COMPLETE wellness between episodes with normal growth, often with a personal/family history of migraine, typically resolving or evolving into typical migraine in later childhood/adolescence. It is largely a CLINICAL diagnosis of EXCLUSION, made after history/examination and exclusion of red flags and other causes \u2014 which is why investigations are normal: normal tests SUPPORT rather than undermine the diagnosis, and explaining this prevents the family feeling nothing has been found. Screen the RED FLAGS (Rome IV alarm features) that would not fit abdominal migraine: weight loss/faltering growth, pain or vomiting waking the child from sleep/nocturnal symptoms, bilious vomiting (an emergency — possible malrotation/obstruction) or persistent vomiting, GI bleeding, chronic severe diarrhoea, fever, persistent right-sided/localised pain, abnormal examination, dysuria, joint/perianal/systemic features, family history of IBD, or symptoms between attacks — any of which warrant assessment/investigation. On the telephone, arrange a face-to-face review to examine her, plot growth and test urine. Consider the differential for recurrent paediatric abdominal pain (constipation, UTI, coeliac disease, inflammatory bowel disease, surgical causes such as intermittent obstruction/intussusception, intermittent pelviureteric junction obstruction, cyclical vomiting syndrome, mesenteric adenitis, and functional abdominal pain/IBS) and use appropriate basic tests (coeliac serology per NICE NG20, urine per NICE NG224; constipation per NICE CG99) to exclude. Manage with reassurance and clear explanation (a real form of migraine, not "nothing"), trigger identification/avoidance (stress, sleep, food, dehydration), lifestyle measures, acute symptomatic treatment (rest in a quiet dark room, early paracetamol or ibuprofen per BNFC, hydration; antiemetics are not routine at this age — domperidone not licensed under 12, MHRA December 2019; metoclopramide restricted in under-18s, MHRA August 2013 — and triptans are not licensed at 8), a symptom diary, and paediatric referral for frequent or severe episodes (preventive medicines are a specialist decision). The skill is recognising the stereotyped episodic pattern with symptom-free intervals, explaining why normal tests fit, excluding red flags, and managing/reassuring — not endless re-investigation. No NICE NG12 (updated April 2026) cancer pathway applies.',
    knowledge:{
      guideline:'Abdominal migraine (paediatric migraine variant) · stereotyped episodic central pain + symptom-free intervals · diagnosis of exclusion · red flags · trigger/acute management · [1] International Classification of Headache Disorders, 3rd edition, 1.6.1.2 Abdominal migraine (International Headache Society 2018, international) · [2] Rome IV childhood functional gastrointestinal disorders, Hyams et al., Gastroenterology 2016 (international) · [3] NICE NG20 Coeliac disease (2015) · [4] NICE NG224 Urinary tract infection in under 16s (2022) · [5] NICE CG99 Constipation in children and young people (2010) · [6] MHRA Drug Safety Update: domperidone (December 2019) and metoclopramide (August 2013) · [7] BNFC',
      points:[
        { h:'Recognise', t:'Recurrent stereotyped central abdominal pain (hours: 2–72 h untreated per ICHD-3 [1]; at least 1 h per Rome IV [2]) with pallor, nausea/vomiting, anorexia, lethargy; complete wellness between; normal growth; often migraine history. Resolves/evolves into typical migraine.' },
        { h:'Diagnosis of exclusion', t:'Largely clinical — made after excluding red flags/other causes, with a face-to-face examination, growth and urine. Normal investigations support, not undermine, the diagnosis. Explain this to the family.' },
        { h:'Red flags', t:'Weight loss/faltering growth, nocturnal/waking or persistent vomiting, bilious vomiting (emergency), GI bleeding, fever, localised/RIF pain, abnormal exam, dysuria, family history of IBD, symptoms between attacks → assess/investigate [2].' },
        { h:'Differential', t:'Constipation [5], UTI [4], coeliac [3], IBD, surgical causes (intermittent obstruction/malrotation/intussusception), intermittent PUJ obstruction, cyclical vomiting syndrome, mesenteric adenitis, functional abdominal pain/IBS. Use coeliac serology/urine to exclude.' },
        { h:'Management', t:'Reassurance/explanation, trigger identification/avoidance (stress, sleep, food, dehydration), lifestyle, acute treatment (quiet dark room, early paracetamol/ibuprofen per BNFC [7], hydration; no routine antiemetic [6]; no triptan at 8), symptom diary.' },
        { h:'Frequent/severe', t:'Paediatric referral for frequent or severe episodes; preventive medicines are a specialist decision.' },
        { h:'Never do', t:'Never endlessly re-investigate once the pattern is clear and red flags excluded; never label it without excluding red flags/mimics; never dismiss it as "nothing" because tests are normal.' },
        { h:'Safety-net & follow-up', t:'999/A&E for green vomiting; urgent review if pattern changes, weight loss, localised/nocturnal pain, GI bleeding, or unwell between attacks; trigger/acute management; diary; paediatric referral if frequent/severe.' }
      ]
    }
